Composition
Compose::beside(), Compose::stack(), and Compose::grid() lay out separate plots as patchwork does for ggplot2. A composition takes a title, a theme, and Vega-Lite resolve rules for shared or independent scales and legends.
use Phiz\Compose;
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$faithful = Datasets::faithful();

$plot = Compose::beside(
    Plot::of($faithful)-&gt;aes(x: 'eruptions', y: 'waiting')-&gt;point(alpha: 0.5)-&gt;size(280, 240),
    Plot::of($faithful)-&gt;aes(x: 'waiting')-&gt;histogram(binwidth: 3)-&gt;coordFlip()-&gt;size(120, 240),
)-&gt;title('Old Faithful: eruption length and waiting time');

Selections defined in one plot of a composition can filter or highlight the others. Interaction shows a linked brush.

The charts
fuel_dashboard() takes the cars table and the filter state and returns three plots and the class summary behind the bar chart. It lives in examples/dashboard.php, and the Livewire component below calls it from render().

The bar chart shows mean highway mpg by class, from group_by('class') |&gt; summarise(...). The selected class is highlighted with a computed field.
The scatterplot shows the filtered cars, with one linear fit across all of them.
The trend chart compares each manufacturer’s mean in 1999 and 2008. It ignores the year filter, so the comparison stays visible.

/*
 * The fuel economy dashboard from the "Dashboards with Livewire" documentation page: three charts built from phplyr
 * summaries of one filtered phibble table. A Livewire component calls fuel_dashboard() in render() with its public
 * properties, so every chart follows the same filters.
 */

use Phibble\Contracts\Table;
use Phiz\Plot;

use function Phiz\aes;

use function Phplyr\{arrange, desc, filter, group_by, mean, n, summarise};

/**
 * @param string|null $drive 'f', 'r', '4', or null for all
 * @param int|null $year 1999, 2008, or null for both
 * @return array{summary: Table, bars: Plot, scatter: Plot, trend: Plot}
 */
function fuel_dashboard(Table $cars, ?string $drive = null, ?int $year = null, ?string $selected = null): array
{
    $byDrive = $cars |&gt; filter(fn ($r) =&gt; $drive === null || $r-&gt;drv === $drive);
    $subset = $byDrive |&gt; filter(fn ($r) =&gt; $year === null || $r-&gt;year === $year);

    $byClass = $subset
        |&gt; group_by('class')
        |&gt; summarise(cars: n(), hwy: mean('hwy'), cty: mean('cty'))
        |&gt; arrange(desc('hwy'));

    // The trend compares the two years, so it ignores the year filter.
    $byManufacturer = $byDrive
        |&gt; group_by('manufacturer', 'year')
        |&gt; summarise(hwy: mean('hwy'));

    $bars = Plot::of($byClass)
        -&gt;aes(x: 'hwy', y: \Phiz\field('class', sort: '-x'), fill: \Phiz\calc('datum.class === '.json_encode($selected ?? ''), 'selected', 'nominal'))
        -&gt;col()
        -&gt;scaleFill(domain: [false, true], range: ['#9ECAE1', '#08519C'], guide: false)
        -&gt;tooltip(['class', 'cars', 'hwy', 'cty'])
        -&gt;labs(title: 'Mean highway mpg by class', x: 'Highway mpg', y: null)
        -&gt;size(320, 220);

    $scatter = Plot::of($subset)
        -&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy', color: 'class')
        -&gt;point(alpha: 0.7, size: 40)
        -&gt;smooth(aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy'), method: 'lm', color: '#333333', inheritAes: false)
        -&gt;tooltip(['manufacturer', 'model', 'year', 'displ', 'hwy'])
        -&gt;theme(legendPosition: 'bottom')
        -&gt;labs(title: 'Displacement and highway mpg', x: 'Displacement (L)', y: 'Highway mpg', color: null)
        -&gt;size(320, 220);

    $trend = Plot::of($byManufacturer)
        -&gt;aes(x: 'year:O', y: 'hwy', group: 'manufacturer')
        -&gt;line(color: '#BBBBBB')
        -&gt;point(color: '#666666', size: 20)
        -&gt;tooltip(['manufacturer', 'year', 'hwy'])
        -&gt;labs(title: 'Manufacturers, 1999 to 2008', x: null, y: 'Mean highway mpg')
        -&gt;size(200, 220);

    return ['summary' =&gt; $byClass, 'bars' =&gt; $bars, 'scatter' =&gt; $scatter, 'trend' =&gt; $trend];
}
With the drive filter on front-wheel drive, the year on 2008, and the compact class selected, the charts look like this:
use Phibble\DataFrame;
use Phiz\Compose;
use Phiz\Datasets;

require_once __DIR__.'/../../examples/dashboard.php';

// The dashboard with drive = 'f', year = 2008, and the compact class selected.
$charts = fuel_dashboard(DataFrame::fromRows(Datasets::mpg()), drive: 'f', year: 2008, selected: 'compact');

$plot = Compose::beside($charts['bars'], $charts['scatter'], $charts['trend']);

The Livewire component
The component keeps the filters as public properties. render() passes them to fuel_dashboard(). Livewire calls render() after every update, so a new selection produces new specifications, and the phiz loader re-embeds each chart whose specification changed.
namespace App\Livewire;

use Livewire\Attributes\Url;
use Livewire\Component;
use Phibble\DataFrame;
use Phiz\Datasets;

class FuelDashboard extends Component
{
    #[Url]
    public ?string $drive = null;

    #[Url]
    public ?int $year = null;

    public ?string $selected = null;

    public function select(string $class): void
    {
        $this-&gt;selected = $this-&gt;selected === $class ? null : $class;
    }

    public function render()
    {
        // In an application this would be DataFrame::fromQuery(Car::query()) or a cached table.
        $cars = DataFrame::fromRows(Datasets::mpg());

        return view('livewire.fuel-dashboard', fuel_dashboard($cars, $this-&gt;drive, $this-&gt;year, $this-&gt;selected));
    }
}
The view puts the filters and the three charts side by side. Each &lt;x-phiz&gt; has a wire:key, so Livewire matches each chart to its element across renders.
&lt;div class="space-y-4"&gt;
    &lt;div class="flex gap-4"&gt;
        &lt;select wire:model.live="drive"&gt;
            &lt;option value=""&gt;All drives&lt;/option&gt;
            &lt;option value="f"&gt;Front&lt;/option&gt;
            &lt;option value="r"&gt;Rear&lt;/option&gt;
            &lt;option value="4"&gt;4WD&lt;/option&gt;
        &lt;/select&gt;
        &lt;select wire:model.live="year"&gt;
            &lt;option value=""&gt;Both years&lt;/option&gt;
            &lt;option value="1999"&gt;1999&lt;/option&gt;
            &lt;option value="2008"&gt;2008&lt;/option&gt;
        &lt;/select&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-4"&gt;
        &lt;x-phiz :plot="$bars" wire:key="bars" /&gt;
        &lt;x-phiz :plot="$scatter" wire:key="scatter" /&gt;
        &lt;x-phiz :plot="$trend" wire:key="trend" /&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;

    &lt;ul class="flex gap-2 text-sm"&gt;
        @foreach ($summary-&gt;toRows() as $row)
            &lt;li&gt;&lt;button wire:click="select('{{ $row['class'] }}')"&gt;{{ $row['class'] }} ({{ $row['cars'] }})&lt;/button&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
        @endforeach
    &lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
A click on a chart can also drive the component. The loader dispatches a phiz:rendered event with the Vega view, and a small Alpine handler can forward selections with $wire:
&lt;div x-on:phiz:rendered="$event.detail.view.addEventListener('click', (e, item) =&gt; item?.datum?.class && $wire.select(item.datum.class))"&gt;
    &lt;x-phiz :plot="$bars" wire:key="bars" /&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

Linking in the browser
When the data is small enough to send to the page, a composition links charts without a request to the server. A brush on the scatterplot filters the bar chart, and Vega-Lite recomputes the means in the browser.
use Phiz\Compose;
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$mpg = Datasets::mpg();

// Linked in the browser: brushing the scatterplot filters the bars, with no request to the server.
$plot = Compose::beside(
    Plot::of($mpg)-&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy', color: 'drv')-&gt;point(alpha: 0.7)-&gt;brush(encodings: ['x', 'y'])-&gt;size(320, 240),
    Plot::of($mpg)-&gt;aes(x: 'mean(hwy)', y: \Phiz\field('class', sort: '-x'))-&gt;col(filter: ['param' =&gt; 'brush'], fill: '#4C78A8')-&gt;labs(x: 'Mean highway mpg', y: null)-&gt;size(260, 240),
);

Aggregates and time units
Shorthand also covers Vega-Lite’s aggregates and time units: 'mean(hwy)', 'count()', 'median(price)', 'year(date):O', and 'yearmonth(date)'. The aggregation runs in Vega-Lite, grouped by the other mapped fields.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::economics())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'year(date):O', y: 'mean(psavert)')
    -&gt;col(fill: '#4C78A8')
    -&gt;labs(x: null, y: 'Mean personal savings rate (%)');

Computed fields
calc() maps an aesthetic to a field computed from a Vega expression. The expression refers to the row as datum.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

use function Phiz\calc;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'cty', y: calc('datum.hwy - datum.cty', as: 'gap'))
    -&gt;jitter(width: 0.3, height: 0.3, alpha: 0.5)
    -&gt;labs(x: 'City mpg', y: 'Highway minus city mpg');

Distributions
histogram() bins a continuous variable. bins: sets the maximum number of bins, and Vega-Lite chooses rounded boundaries. binwidth: sets the width exactly and boundary: anchors the bins.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::faithful())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'waiting')
    -&gt;histogram(binwidth: 2, fill: '#4C78A8')
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Minutes between eruptions');

density() draws a kernel density estimate per group with Vega-Lite’s density transform. bw: sets the bandwidth. adjust: multiplies a bandwidth computed with R’s bw.nrd0 rule. Mapping fill draws filled areas; otherwise the densities are lines.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'hwy', fill: 'drv')
    -&gt;density(alpha: 0.4, adjust: 1.5)
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Highway mpg', fill: 'Drive');

boxplot() draws Tukey boxplots with whiskers at coef: interquartile ranges. Points layered on top show the raw data.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'drv', y: 'hwy')
    -&gt;boxplot(outliers: false, fill: '#DDDDDD', color: '#333333')
    -&gt;jitter(width: 0.25, alpha: 0.35)
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Drive', y: 'Highway mpg');

violin() draws a kernel density of y for each level of a discrete x. Every violin has the same maximum width, set by width: as a share of the band, and violins layer with boxplots and points on the same axis. For inline data, each violin’s density is computed in PHP as ggplot2 computes it: its own bandwidth (bw:, or R’s bw.nrd0 times adjust:), evaluated at n: points (200 by default) across the group’s range. position: 'dodge' places groups side by side within the band.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'drv', y: 'hwy', fill: 'drv')
    -&gt;violin(alpha: 0.6, adjust: 1.2)
    -&gt;jitter(width: 0.08, alpha: 0.3)
    -&gt;theme('gray')
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Drive', y: 'Highway mpg', fill: 'Drive');

ecdf(), freqpoly(), qq(), qqLine(), and rug() complete the set.

Amounts
bar() counts rows per level of x when y is unmapped, and col() draws the y values. position: is stack (the default), dodge, fill (stacked to 100%), or identity.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'class', fill: 'drv')
    -&gt;bar(position: 'dodge')
    -&gt;labs(x: null, y: 'Cars', fill: 'Drive');

col() with xmin and xmax mapped draws precomputed bins. tile() and rect() draw heatmaps and rectangles, and bin2d() counts points in a two-dimensional grid.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

use function Phiz\factor;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'class', y: factor('cyl'), fill: 'mean(hwy)')
    -&gt;tile(color: 'white')
    -&gt;scaleFill(scheme: 'viridis')
    -&gt;labs(x: null, y: 'Cylinders', fill: 'Mean hwy');

Lines and areas
line() connects points in order of x. path() connects them in data order, and step() draws stairsteps. area() fills from zero and stacks by fill, and ribbon() fills between ymin and ymax.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$recent = array_slice(Datasets::economics(), -180);

$plot = Plot::of($recent)
    -&gt;aes(x: 'date', y: 'uempmed')
    -&gt;area(fill: '#9ECAE1', alpha: 0.5)
    -&gt;line(color: '#08519C')
    -&gt;labs(x: null, y: 'Median weeks unemployed');

Text
text() and label() draw the label aesthetic. hjust, vjust, and angle follow ggplot2: angle turns text counterclockwise. nudgeX and nudgeY move labels in pixels.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$fast = array_values(array_filter(Datasets::mtcars(), fn ($car) =&gt; $car['hp'] &gt; 180));

$plot = Plot::of($fast)
    -&gt;aes(x: 'hp', y: 'qsec', label: 'model')
    -&gt;point()
    -&gt;text(hjust: 0, nudgeX: 6, size: 10)
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Horsepower', y: 'Quarter-mile time (s)');

Fitted curves
smooth() fits a curve per group and, for inline data, draws a pointwise confidence band for the mean at level:, as ggplot2’s geom_smooth() does. The default method is loess: a local quadratic fit over the nearest span: share of the points (0.75 by default). phiz fits it in PHP, and the fit, its standard errors, and the t interval match R’s loess(y ~ x, span = 0.75, degree = 2) with surface = "direct" to within 1e-9. ggplot2’s own default interpolates the loess surface, so its curve can differ from R’s direct fit in the third decimal place.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy')
    -&gt;point(alpha: 0.4)
    -&gt;smooth(span: 0.75)
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Displacement (L)', y: 'Highway mpg');

method: 'lm', 'quad', and 'poly' (with order:) fit polynomials by least squares, and their bands match R’s predict(fit, interval = "confidence"). se: false and the other methods (log, exp, pow) use Vega-Lite’s regression and loess transforms in the browser, without a band. Above 3,000 rows a loess smooth also defaults to the browser, because the band costs O(n²) in PHP. Pass se: true to compute it anyway.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mtcars())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'hp', y: 'mpg')
    -&gt;point()
    -&gt;smooth(method: 'poly', order: 2, level: 0.9)
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Horsepower', y: 'mpg');

curve() draws a PHP function over a range, as ggplot2’s geom_function() does.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$eruptions = array_column(Datasets::faithful(), 'eruptions');
$mean = array_sum($eruptions) / count($eruptions);
$sd = sqrt(array_sum(array_map(fn ($x) =&gt; ($x - $mean) ** 2, $eruptions)) / (count($eruptions) - 1));
$normal = fn (float $x) =&gt; exp(-0.5 * (($x - $mean) / $sd) ** 2) / ($sd * sqrt(2 * M_PI));

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::faithful())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'eruptions')
    -&gt;histogram(binwidth: 0.2, density: true, fill: '#DDDDDD')
    -&gt;curve($normal, 1.5, 5.5, aes: ['x' =&gt; 'eruptions', 'y' =&gt; 'density'], color: '#D62728')
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Eruption length (min)', subtitle: 'A single normal curve fits this bimodal distribution poorly');

Summaries
summary() summarizes y at each value of x and draws the result with another geom, as ggplot2’s stat_summary() does. funData: returns the middle and the range together:

mean_se (the default): the mean, plus or minus one standard error (funArgs: ['mult' =&gt; 2] for two)
mean_cl_normal: the mean with a t confidence interval (funArgs: ['conf' =&gt; 0.9])
mean_sdl: the mean, plus or minus two standard deviations
median_hilow: the median with the outer quantiles at conf (0.95 by default)

A callable can take the place of any of them, returning [y, ymin, ymax]. fun:, funMin:, and funMax: set the parts separately, by name (mean, median, min, max, sum, sd, n) or as callables of the group’s values. geom: chooses how to draw the summaries: pointrange (the default), errorbar, linerange, crossbar, point, line, col, area, or ribbon. With a discrete y and a continuous x, x is summarized at each y instead.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'class', y: 'hwy')
    -&gt;point(alpha: 0.25)
    -&gt;summary(funData: 'mean_cl_normal', color: '#D62728')
    -&gt;labs(x: null, y: 'Highway mpg', title: 'Means with 95% t intervals');

crossbar() draws a box from ymin to ymax with a line at y, and it works with summary() and position: 'dodge'.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'class', y: 'hwy', fill: 'drv')
    -&gt;summary(funData: 'median_hilow', funArgs: ['conf' =&gt; 0.5], geom: 'crossbar', position: 'dodge')
    -&gt;labs(x: null, y: 'Highway mpg', title: 'Medians and quartiles');

count() draws a point at each distinct x and y, sized by the number of rows there, as geom_count() does.
ellipse() draws a confidence ellipse per group, as stat_ellipse() does. type: 't' (the default) fits a multivariate t distribution with MASS’s cov.trob(), 'norm' uses the sample covariance, and 'euclid' draws a circle of radius level. The ellipses match ggplot2’s to within 1e-9. geom: 'polygon' fills them.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$faithful = array_map(fn ($row) =&gt; $row + ['wait' =&gt; $row['waiting'] &gt; 70 ? 'long' : 'short'], Datasets::faithful());

$plot = Plot::of($faithful)
    -&gt;aes(x: 'eruptions', y: 'waiting', color: 'wait')
    -&gt;point(alpha: 0.6)
    -&gt;ellipse()
    -&gt;ellipse(type: 'norm', linetype: 'dashed')
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Eruption (minutes)', y: 'Waiting time (minutes)', color: 'Wait');

Two-dimensional densities and contours
density2d() estimates a bivariate kernel density on a 100 × 100 grid and draws its contour lines, as geom_density_2d() does. The density is MASS’s kde2d() with bandwidth.nrd() bandwidths, computed in PHP, and the contour levels are R’s pretty() over the range of the density. h: sets the bandwidths, adjust: multiplies them, and bins:, binwidth:, or breaks: set the levels. Mapping color: 'level' colors each line by its level. Mapping color to a field estimates one density per group.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::faithful())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'eruptions', y: 'waiting')
    -&gt;point(size: 10, alpha: 0.6)
    -&gt;density2d()
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Eruption (minutes)', y: 'Waiting time (minutes)');

density2dFilled() fills the bands between consecutive levels and labels them as ggplot2 does, with the viridis palette. contourVar: 'ndensity' scales each group’s density to a maximum of 1, and 'count' multiplies it by the group’s size.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::faithful())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'eruptions', y: 'waiting')
    -&gt;density2dFilled()
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Eruption (minutes)', y: 'Waiting time (minutes)', fill: 'density');

contour() and contourFilled() draw the same lines and bands for a z field on a regular grid of x and y, as geom_contour() and geom_contour_filled() do. phiz traces the contours in PHP with marching squares. The lines match ggplot2’s: the same levels and the same pieces.
use Phiz\Plot;

// z on a regular grid of x and y.
$grid = [];
foreach (range(-3, 3, 0.2) as $x) {
    foreach (range(-3, 3, 0.2) as $y) {
        $grid[] = ['x' =&gt; $x, 'y' =&gt; $y, 'z' =&gt; sin($x) * cos($y) + 0.1 * $x * $y];
    }
}

$plot = Plot::of($grid)
    -&gt;aes(x: 'x', y: 'y', z: 'z')
    -&gt;contourFilled(bins: 7)
    -&gt;contour(bins: 7, color: 'white', linewidth: 0.5);

polygon() fills the shape through each group’s points in data order. Rows with null x and y start a new ring in the same group, and a ring drawn in the opposite direction cuts a hole.

Tooltips, pan, and zoom
tooltip() adds tooltips to every layer. With no argument the tooltip shows the encoded fields, and a list of fields shows those fields. interactive() binds the scales to dragging and scrolling.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mtcars())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'wt', y: 'mpg', color: 'hp')
    -&gt;point(size: 70)
    -&gt;tooltip(['model', 'mpg', 'wt', 'hp'])
    -&gt;interactive()
    -&gt;scaleColor(scheme: 'plasma');

Brushing and linked views
brush() adds a drag-to-select interval named brush. Marks outside the selection turn gray. Another layer, or another plot in a composition, can react with filter: ['param' =&gt; 'brush']. Brush the scatterplot to filter the bar chart:
use Phiz\Compose;
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$mpg = Datasets::mpg();

$plot = Compose::stack(
    Plot::of($mpg)-&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy', color: 'drv')-&gt;point()-&gt;brush(encodings: ['x', 'y'])-&gt;size(420, 220),
    Plot::of($mpg)-&gt;aes(x: 'class', fill: 'drv')-&gt;bar(filter: ['param' =&gt; 'brush'])-&gt;size(420, 140),
);

Legend highlighting
highlight() binds a selection to the legend of an aesthetic. Clicking an entry fades the other groups. Shift-click selects several.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

$long = [];
foreach (Datasets::economics() as $row) {
    if ($row['date'] &gt;= '2000-01-01') {
        $long[] = ['date' =&gt; $row['date'], 'series' =&gt; 'Savings rate (%)', 'value' =&gt; $row['psavert']];
        $long[] = ['date' =&gt; $row['date'], 'series' =&gt; 'Median weeks unemployed', 'value' =&gt; $row['uempmed']];
    }
}

$plot = Plot::of($long)
    -&gt;aes(x: 'date', y: 'value', color: 'series')
    -&gt;line(linewidth: 2)
    -&gt;highlight()
    -&gt;labs(x: null, y: null, color: 'Click a series');

Sliders and inputs
slider() and dropdown() add parameters bound to inputs. Expressions in filters and expr() values read them by name.
use Phiz\Datasets;
use Phiz\Plot;

use function Phiz\expr;

$plot = Plot::of(Datasets::mpg())
    -&gt;aes(x: 'displ', y: 'hwy')
    -&gt;slider('cutoff', 15, 40, step: 1, value: 30, label: 'Highway mpg cutoff')
    -&gt;point(filter: 'datum.hwy &gt;= cutoff', color: '#D62728')
    -&gt;point(filter: 'datum.hwy &lt; cutoff', color: '#BBBBBB')
    -&gt;hline(expr('cutoff'), linetype: 'dashed');

param() and select() add any Vega-Lite parameter.

Binning in PHP
hex(), bin2d(), and histogram() count in PHP and embed only the counts. hex() always bins in PHP. bin2d() and histogram() take compute:, which is 'server', 'browser', or 'auto'. The default 'auto' bins in PHP above Config::$serverRows rows (10,000 by default, or config('phiz.server_rows')). The examples on this page use 200,000 points.
use Phiz\Plot;

$points = require __DIR__.'/_cloud.php'; // 200,000 rows as columns

$plot = Plot::of($points)
    -&gt;aes(x: 'x', y: 'y')
    -&gt;hex(bins: 40)
    -&gt;scaleFill(scheme: 'viridis')
    -&gt;labs(title: '200,000 points in hexagonal bins');

The hexagons are sized from the panel width, so neighbors tile at any plot size. A faceted plot bins each panel separately, and the specification still holds only the cells.
use Phiz\Plot;

$points = require __DIR__.'/_cloud.php';

// Above Config::$serverRows rows, bin2d() counts in PHP; the spec holds only the cells.
$plot = Plot::of($points)
    -&gt;aes(x: 'x', y: 'y')
    -&gt;bin2d(bins: 40)
    -&gt;facetWrap('group')
    -&gt;size(240, 220)
    -&gt;scaleFill(scheme: 'blues');

Histograms computed in PHP use exactly bins equal-width bins, or binwidth and boundary, and stack or overlap by group as usual.
use Phiz\Plot;

$points = require __DIR__.'/_cloud.php';

$plot = Plot::of($points)
    -&gt;aes(x: 'x', fill: 'group')
    -&gt;histogram(bins: 60, position: 'identity', alpha: 0.6)
    -&gt;labs(fill: null);

Downsampling lines
line(downsample: n) keeps about n points per group with Largest-Triangle-Three-Buckets (LTTB). LTTB picks the points that preserve the line’s visible shape, including its peaks.
use Phiz\Plot;

// A 100,000-point random walk, drawn from 800 points chosen by Largest-Triangle-Three-Buckets.
$rng = new Random\Randomizer(new Random\Engine\Mt19937(7));
$walk = [];
$level = 0.0;
for ($t = 0; $t &lt; 100_000; $t++) {
    $level += $rng-&gt;getFloat(-1, 1);
    $walk[] = ['t' =&gt; $t, 'level' =&gt; $level];
}

$plot = Plot::of($walk)
    -&gt;aes(x: 't', y: 'level')
    -&gt;line(downsample: 800, color: '#1F77B4')
    -&gt;size(560, 220);

The Bin helpers
The Bin class exposes the same computations for data you summarize yourself. Each helper returns a short list of rows. The helpers read phibble tables, column arrays, row lists, or any iterable of rows. A generator streams in one pass when the limits are given.
use Phiz\Bin;

Bin::histogram($source, 'price', bins: 50);          // price_start, price_end, count, density
Bin::grid($source, 'x', 'y', bins: [40, 30]);         // x_start, x_end, y_start, y_end, count
Bin::hex($source, 'x', 'y', bins: 30, aspect: 0.75);  // hexagon centers and count
Bin::lttb($rows, 'time', 'value', threshold: 1000);   // the rows LTTB keeps
Bin::sample($source, 5000, seed: 1);                  // a reproducible uniform sample

// One pass over a database cursor, with known limits:
$bins = Bin::grid(Car::query()-&gt;cursor(), 'weight', 'mpg', bins: 40, xlim: [1500, 5500], ylim: [5, 50]);

Plot::of($bins)
    -&gt;rect(aes(xmin: 'weight_start', xmax: 'weight_end', ymin: 'mpg_start', ymax: 'mpg_end', fill: 'count'));

Static export
svg(), png(), pdf(), and save() run the vl-convert command-line tool. It renders without a browser, which suits reports, email, and print. vl-convert bundles its own Vega-Lite, and phiz runs it with Vega-Lite 6.1. The test suite validates every specification against the Vega-Lite 6.1 schema, so the same specification renders in both places.
$plot-&gt;save('figure.svg');
$plot-&gt;save('figure.png', scale: 3);
file_put_contents('figure.pdf', $plot-&gt;pdf());
phiz finds the binary through the PHIZ_VL_CONVERT or VL_CONVERT environment variable, then PATH. Download it from the vl-convert releases. Version 1.9.0 is tested. If the binary is missing, phiz throws Phiz\Exceptions\VlConvertNotFound with these instructions. new Export(binary: ..., timeout: ..., fontDir: ...) sets the binary path, the timeout, and an extra font directory explicitly.

Coefficient plots
coefplot() draws estimates with confidence intervals and a reference line at no effect. It drops the intercept unless intercept: true. It uses the fit’s conf_int by default, and level: recomputes the intervals from the standard errors with t or normal quantiles to match the fit.
use function Phiz\coefplot;

// The fit as php-stats returns it: stats_lm('mpg ~ wt + hp + am', $mtcars).
$fit = json_decode(file_get_contents(__DIR__.'/fits/mtcars-lm.json'), true);

$plot = coefplot($fit)-&gt;labs(title: 'mpg ~ wt + hp + am');

Several fits keyed by label are compared side by side, with one color per model. sort: true orders terms by estimate, and terms: selects and orders them.
use function Phiz\coefplot;

$full = json_decode(file_get_contents(__DIR__.'/fits/mtcars-lm.json'), true);
$reduced = json_decode(file_get_contents(__DIR__.'/fits/mtcars-lm-reduced.json'), true);

$plot = coefplot(['mpg ~ wt + hp + am' =&gt; $full, 'mpg ~ wt + hp' =&gt; $reduced]);

exponentiate: true shows odds or rate ratios from a GLM on a log scale with the reference at 1. The axis is labeled by the fit’s link: log odds or odds ratios for logit models, and log rates or rate ratios for log links.
use function Phiz\coefplot;

// stats_glm('am ~ wt + hp', $mtcars, 'binomial')
$glm = json_decode(file_get_contents(__DIR__.'/fits/mtcars-glm.json'), true);

$plot = coefplot($glm, exponentiate: true)-&gt;labs(x: 'Odds ratio (95% CI)');

Regression diagnostics
residual_plot(), qq_plot(), scale_location_plot(), and fitted_plot() draw the standard diagnostic panels. diagnostic_plots() arranges all four in a grid.
use function Phiz\diagnostic_plots;

// The fit as php-stats returns it: stats_lm('mpg ~ wt + hp + am', $mtcars).
$fit = json_decode(file_get_contents(__DIR__.'/fits/mtcars-lm.json'), true);

$plot = diagnostic_plots($fit, width: 240, height: 180);

qq_plot() also accepts a plain list of values. The QQ geoms place the sorted sample at the normal quantiles of R’s ppoints(n), per group, as qqnorm() and ggplot2’s stat_qq() do. The reference line passes through the sample quartiles, as qqline() and stat_qq_line() draw it.

Predicted values
effect_plot() draws predictions with confidence bands as one variable varies across its observed range. by: draws one line per level of a grouping variable. Other variables are held at their mean, or at their most common value when they are categorical.
For formula fits, phiz builds the prediction design with stats_model_matrix() on the fitting data and the grid together, so factor levels and reference categories match the fit. For matrix fits, pass design:, a function from a grid row to a design row. GLM intervals are computed on the link scale and transformed to the response scale. The predictions match R’s predict() with interval = "confidence".
use Phiz\Datasets;

use function Phiz\effect_plot;

// The fit as php-stats returns it: stats_lm('mpg ~ wt + hp + am', $mtcars).
$fit = json_decode(file_get_contents(__DIR__.'/fits/mtcars-lm.json'), true);

// Formula fits build the prediction design with stats_model_matrix(); here we pass it directly.
$plot = effect_plot($fit, Datasets::mtcars(), 'wt', by: 'am', design: fn (array $row) =&gt; [1, $row['wt'], $row['hp'], $row['am']])
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Weight (1000 lbs)', y: 'mpg', color: 'Manual', fill: 'Manual');

use Phiz\Datasets;

use function Phiz\effect_plot;

// stats_glm('am ~ wt + hp', $mtcars, 'binomial'): predictions on the probability scale.
$glm = json_decode(file_get_contents(__DIR__.'/fits/mtcars-glm.json'), true);

$plot = effect_plot($glm, Datasets::mtcars(), 'wt', design: fn (array $row) =&gt; [1, $row['wt'], $row['hp']])
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Weight (1000 lbs)', y: 'P(manual transmission)');

scale: 'link' draws predictions on the link scale instead, where a GLM is linear in its predictors:
use Phiz\Datasets;

use function Phiz\effect_plot;

// The same logistic fit, on the log-odds scale, where the model is linear.
$glm = json_decode(file_get_contents(__DIR__.'/fits/mtcars-glm.json'), true);

$plot = effect_plot($glm, Datasets::mtcars(), 'wt', design: fn (array $row) =&gt; [1, $row['wt'], $row['hp']], scale: 'link')
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Weight (1000 lbs)');

predictions($fit, $grid) returns the predicted rows without plotting them, and takes the same scale: option.

Marginal means
marginal_means() estimates the mean response at each level of a factor, as R’s emmeans package does. For each level it averages the predictions with equal weight over the levels of the model’s other categorical variables, with numeric variables at their means. The interval comes from the averaged design row. by: estimates the means within each level of a second factor. For a GLM, the means and intervals are computed on the link scale and transformed to the response scale, as emmeans’ type = "response" does.
The model’s variables come from its formula: names inside factor() and non-numeric columns are categorical. For a matrix fit, pass design: and variables: ['cyl' =&gt; 'categorical', 'wt' =&gt; 'numeric'].
use Phiz\Datasets;

use function Phiz\marginal_means;

// stats_lm('mpg ~ factor(cyl) + factor(am) + wt', $mtcars)
$fit = json_decode(file_get_contents(__DIR__.'/fits/mtcars-lm-factors.json'), true);

$plot = marginal_means($fit, Datasets::mtcars(), 'cyl', by: 'am')
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Cylinders', color: 'Manual');

use Phiz\Datasets;

use function Phiz\marginal_means;

// stats_glm('am ~ factor(cyl) + wt', $mtcars, 'binomial'): means on the probability scale.
$glm = json_decode(file_get_contents(__DIR__.'/fits/mtcars-glm-factor.json'), true);

$plot = marginal_means($glm, Datasets::mtcars(), 'cyl')
    -&gt;labs(x: 'Cylinders', y: 'P(manual), marginal mean (95% CI)');

The linear-model means match a direct computation in R to 10 digits. GLM means match too. Their intervals differ from R’s by about 1e-5, because R’s vcov() for a GLM uses the weights of the iteration before convergence, while php-stats evaluates the information matrix at the final estimates.

Random effects
random_effects_plot() draws a caterpillar plot of a mixed model’s random effects. It works with fits from stats_lmer and stats_glmer. Current php-stats fits report the conditional variances themselves (effects_cond_var, and random_effects_cond_var for GLMMs), and the plot uses them directly. For older fits, pass the data the model was fitted on, and phiz rebuilds the variances from it. Each group’s conditional mode is shown with an interval from its conditional variance. Groups are sorted by their first effect, and random slopes get their own panels. The conditional covariance for group j is (Zj’WjZj / σ² + G−1)−1, treating the fixed effects and variance components as known, as lme4’s ranef(fit, condVar = TRUE) does. W holds the working weights of binomial and Poisson GLMMs. For intercepts, the standard deviations match lme4 to 1e-8. group: selects a term when the model has several. When the fit is singular, with a variance estimated at zero or random effects perfectly correlated, the subtitle says so. The intervals still follow lme4, which computes them without inverting G.
use Phiz\Datasets;

use function Phiz\random_effects_plot;

// stats_lmer('hwy ~ displ + (1 + displ | manufacturer)', $mpg)
$mixed = json_decode(file_get_contents(__DIR__.'/fits/mpg-lmer-slopes.json'), true);

$plot = random_effects_plot($mixed, Datasets::mpg());

Posterior intervals
interval_plot() draws posterior intervals by variable, as bayesplot’s mcmc_intervals() does. It shows a thick inner interval (50% by default), a thin outer interval (90%), and the median. It takes draws in the shape CmdStanPHP’s draws_columns() returns, or a summary from summary_columns(). Selecting a base name such as theta selects all of its elements. These draws come from the eight schools model, sampled with CmdStanPHP.
use function Phiz\interval_plot;

// Draws as CmdStanPHP's $fit-&gt;draws_columns() returns them: variable =&gt; draws, plus .chain, .iteration, .draw.
$draws = json_decode(file_get_contents(__DIR__.'/fits/eight-schools-draws.json'), true);

$plot = interval_plot($draws, variables: ['mu', 'tau', 'theta']);
